  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday Night through Friday) \nIssued at 1202 AM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nMostly cloudy conditions with the potential for a few breaks in\nthe clouds are anticipated Sunday while weak ridge of high\npressure moves across the southern Plains. This will help temps\nwarm into the 70s for the CWA, while winds remain light. \n\nA low pressure system, still just off the coast of the Pacific\nNorthwest remains progged to move through the Western CONUS\nover the weekend and into the Plains Monday and Tuesday. This \nwave is forecast to join up with shortwave and a surge of tropical\nmoisture moving into the southwest CONUS Sunday/Sunday night before\nit moves into the Plains. The low pressure system and an \nassociated cold front are then forecast to move into/across the \nCWA Monday night and Tuesday. Ahead of the approaching storm \nsystem, gusty southerly winds will help transport precipitable \nwater values in excess of 1.5 inches, which is near climatological\nmax for early November, into the region.\n\nShower and thunderstorm chances return during the day Monday for\nnortheast Oklahoma and then spread over the CWA with the movement\nof the cold front into Tuesday. Deep layer shear values and\nincreasing instability will allow for at least a limited severe\npotential Monday afternoon into Monday night. The greater\npotential looks to be across eastern Oklahoma at this time, though\nif the front/low pressure system arrives faster than more of the\nCWA could be in play. Also, as of now, all modes of severe weather\ncould be possible, as low level shear and low level storm relative\nhelicity looks to increase just ahead of the front Monday evening.\nIn addition to the severe threat, a heavy rain threat that could\ncreate localized flooding conditions also exists with the deep \nmoisture plume setting up over the region. A limiting factor to\nthis is storms could be moving 40-50mph, though any training of\nstorms will increase this threat. \n\nStorm chances taper off with the frontal passage, and then rain \nchances should be ending Tuesday afternoon/evening once the upper \nlevel trof axis clears the region. Behind the frontal boundary, \nbreezy northwest/northerly winds and temperatures more closer to \nthe seasonal average for November are forecast for the second half\nof next week. Another quick moving shortwave looks to push across\nthe Plains late in the week, though with limited moisture to work\nwith will hold onto only slight chances of rain.",
